{"chunk_id": "27cbc6370145", "text": "At the start of the Sauptika Parva, the war is effectively over: Duryodhana lies mortally wounded and only three Kaurava champions remain\u2014Ashvatthama, Kripa, and Kritavarma. Hiding in a forest at night, they grieve the destruction of their side. Unable to sleep, Ashvatthama witnesses an owl silently slaughter sleeping crows and takes it as an omen, inspiring a plan to massacre the Pandava allies while they sleep. Kripa argues for restraint, reflecting on the interplay of destiny and human effort and condemning the killing of sleeping enemies as unrighteous. Ashvatthama, consumed by rage over his father Drona\u2019s death and the perceived injustices of the war, rejects this counsel and vows revenge. The three approach the Pandava camp, but a terrifying supernatural guardian blocks the gate, defeating Ashvatthama\u2019s weapons. Realizing the gravity of his act, he turns to prayer, seeking the aid of Shiva before proceeding.\nAfter Duryodhana\u2019s fall, Ashvatthama, inspired by an owl killing sleeping crows, resolves to massacre the sleeping Pandava allies at night despite Kripa\u2019s counsel.\nThemes: revenge and grief, fate versus human effort, moral decline after war, deception in warfare, omens and inspiration", "vector": [0.0060686818324029446, -0.006589438300579786, -0.006517961621284485, -0.0008657997823320329, -0.010578499175608158, 0.03817517310380936, -0.017698904499411583, -0.01773974858224392, -0.023594001308083534, -0.014635632745921612, 0.027065709233283997, 0.02208278700709343, 0.010374280624091625, 0.0060380492359399796, -0.001769890426658094, 0.004999940283596516, 0.042994722723960876, -0.007004681508988142, 0.03341008350253105, -0.009673131629824638, -0.010183677077293396, 0.015452505089342594, 0.004744667559862137, 0.022409535944461823, 0.002476144814863801, 0.017807820811867714, -0.015193828381597996, -0.024710392579436302, 0.013594119809567928, -0.01907397247850895, -0.011776578612625599, -0.023335324